  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1211 PM CDT Fri Aug 28 2026\n\nSurface high pressure stretched from the eastern Great Lakes\nsouthwest through central IL, continues to provide light winds\nand modest temperatures and humidity for late August. These\ntrends will continue tonight with lows dropping to the lower\n60s.\n\nLater tonight and Saturday, shortwave energy traverses the \nUpper Midwest. Convective allowing models show a cluster of \nstorms tracking southeast across IA Saturday morning, \ndissipating as it reaches the Mississippi River and outruns \nupper support. This will leave central IL with increasing high \nand mid level clouds Saturday afternoon. There is a very low \nchance of a few sprinkles reaching the ground from the mid level\ncloud deck. Tightening MSLP gradient will bring a more \nsustained southerly wind flow than recent days, with the \npotential for a few gusts reaching 15-20 mph into midday before\nthe increasing cloud cover limits mixing. Highs will climb a \nfew degrees to the mid/upper 80s.\n\nWarm advection/isentropic lift aloft increases Saturday night \nand Sunday morning. The current blended forecast remains dry, \nbut this is a period we'll need to watch for a few scattered \nshowers and storms, should trends persist.\n\nHigh confidence remains in a late-season thermal ridge \namplifying across the Mid-Mississippi Valley for much of next\nweek. Deterministic and ensemble guidance align on 500 mb \nheights swelling to 594-596 dam, with 850 mb temperatures \nranging between +22C and +24C. Extreme Forecast Index (EFI) \nvalues from the ECMWF range between 0.80 and 0.85 for max \ntemperatures Monday through Wednesday. This confirms that the \nairmass approaches the top of climatology for late August and \nearly September. That said, a few factors will work to mitigate\nthe heat impacts this time around. (1) Southwest gradient flow \ndominates the period, leading to 10-15 mph winds and higher \ndaytime gusts. (2) Dewpoints are not progged to be excessively \nhigh, mainly mid 60s to around 70F, owing to lower ET at this \npoint in the growing season. (3) Lower sun angle than a month or\ntwo ago. Gridded heat index forecasts peak at 100-105 Mon- Fri.\n\nLower confidence extends to precip chances next week. An active\nring of fire pattern will likely bring repeated MCSs on the\nperiphery of the ridge, which models handle poorly 3+ days out.\nMost guidance keeps this activity to our north until late week,\nwhen the ridge begins to break down, allowing a front to sag in\nfrom the north. Shear is projected to be relatively weak through\nthe period, leading to ML/AI severe outlook probabilities in \nthe 5% category near the boundary.",
